Automated deployment to Minikube in the pipeline

Why: To manage containerized applications at scale with features like load balancing, scaling, and self-healing

4. Infrastructure as Code
Implementation: Defined all infrastructure components (Kubernetes resources) in YAML files

Why: To version control infrastructure and enable reproducible environments

5. Automated Testing
Implementation: Integrated Maven test execution in the build stage (though currently skipped with -DskipTests)

Why: To catch bugs early in the development cycle

6. Artifact Management
Implementation: Pushed Docker images to Docker Hub

Why: To store and version application artifacts for deployment

Breakdown of Your Jenkins Pipeline Stages:
1. Checkout Stage
What: Clones the source code from GitHub

Why: To get the latest version of the code for building and deployment

2. Build Stage
What: Compiles the Java code and packages it into a JAR using Maven

Tools: Maven 3.9.9, JDK 21

Why: To create an executable artifact that can be deployed

3. Docker Build & Push Stage
What:

Builds a Docker image tagged with the build ID

Authenticates with Docker Hub using stored credentials

Pushes the image to Docker Hub

Why: To make the application portable and ready for deployment to any environment

4. Deploy to Minikube Stage
What:

Ensures Minikube is running

Applies Kubernetes manifests to create:

Persistent volume for MySQL

MySQL deployment

Application deployment

Service for network access

Why: To automate the deployment process and ensure consistent environment setup

5. Post-build Cleanup
What: Cleans the workspace after build completion

Why: To free up disk space and maintain a clean environment for subsequent builds

